stic and doesn't meet the scale  of the challenge what we want to do of  course is that windfall taxes offer  people real support 600 pounds for a  third of households off their energy  bills that's the scale of what is  required and whatever the long-term  energy strategy the government unveil it  can't get away from the fact that  they've got to take some action now to  help people i mean something that could  have an impact in the short term is  looking at the supply of oil and gas as  well isn't it and was it the right  decision by the north sea transition  agency to look at this two year  extension of the cambodia's oil fields  license well  domestic gas and oil from the north sea  is a about half our domestic supply at  the minute and that should continue when  no one questions that strategy the  longer term extraction picture for all  fossil fuels around the world has to be  of course governed by you say that but  at the same time labour has previously  opposed the development of  the cambodia oil field and you're  talking about windfall tax in the north  sea as well you know if you were looking  at this you might think that actually  labour isn't that happy about the idea  of what's being calling it it's got to  be governed by the small and short it's  going to be governed by the committee on  climate change there's no point in the  country having is that right does it is  that is that what you think the priority  should be achieving net zero opposed to  bringing bills there's no point this  country having  a ambitious climate strategy if we're  not going to stick to it and of course  even if people face  huge  huge  like difficulties with paying their  bills even if people are having to turn  off their health let's be clear more  domestic supply is about the security of  that supply it's not about the price the  price is set by the global market price  for that so even if we for instance  wanted to go down the route of  extracting a fundamentally larger amount  of oil and gas from the uk that wouldn't  affect the price the only way to affect  the price is to focus on energy  efficiency so people are using less is  to switch to uh domestic cheaper  electrification of heating so it's very  important people understand that point  there is a one thing that's different  about the situation now because of what  russia has done is there's an argument  about substitution of supplies that  would have perhaps come from russia  coming from other parts of the world so  when i say the committee on climate  change has to look at that picture in  the round that is exactly what they will  do but anyone who thinks  bills or supply in the long term can be  sold by more dependents on fossil fuels  that is a mistake you've got to look at  the alternative which is renewables and  nuclear i've got two very short  questions to squeeze in before we have  to let you go firstly david warburton  has had the conservative whip withdrawn  after allegations made in sunday times  now without going into the details  of the case you know he says he's heard  nothing from the  independent complaints and grievance  scheme do you have concerns about the  culture in westminster  well i've read the story and that's all  i know about this particular thing i i  think the vast majority of members of  parliament  work very hard for their constituents  whatever their party affiliation you  know certainly as a front venture for  most of my time in parliament i've been  you know in opposition it's quite  quite a lot of work to make sure you're  matching the capacity the government has  in the civil service i think there are  in any uh sector i've worked in whether  that's the legal sector of politics i  think there are people who sometimes  you know aren't balancing the  responsibilities and the burdens on them  in the right way and they sometimes you  know that gets them into trouble and  behavior they shouldn't do but i don't  think politics is any different in 